2%5Bjour%5D&sort=date&sort_order=desc)\n-   [Search in NLM Catalog](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nlmcatalog?term=%22Cytometry+A%22%5BTitle+Abbreviation%5D)\n-   [Add to Search](#)\n\n. 2022 Jan;101(1):15-20.\n\ndoi: 10.1002/cyto.a.24481. Epub 2021 Jul 14.\n\n# OMIP 077: Definition of all principal human leukocyte populations using a broadly applicable 14-color panel\n\n[Maximilian Boesch](/?term=Boesch+M&cauthor_id=34260151)  [1](#full-view-affiliation-1 \"Lung Center, Cantonal Hospital St.Gallen, St.Gallen, St.Gallen, Switzerland.\") , [Martina Sykora](/?term=Sykora+M&cauthor_id=34260151)  [2](#full-view-affiliation-2 \"Internal Medicine V, Medical University of Innsbruck, Innsbruck, Tyrol, Austria.\")   [3](#full-view-affiliation-3 \"Tyrolean Cancer Research Institute, Innsbruck, Tyrol, Austria.\") , [Silvia Gasteiger](/?term=Gasteiger+S&cauthor_id=34260151)  [3](#full-view-affiliation-3 \"Tyrolean Cancer Research Institute, Innsbruck, Tyrol, Austria.\")   [4](#full-view-affiliation-4 \"Visceral, Transplant and Thoracic Surgery, Medical University of Innsbruck, Innsbruck, Tyrol, Austria.\") , [Florent Baty](/?term=Baty+F&cauthor_id=34260151)  [1](#full-view-affiliation-1 \"Lung Center, Cantonal Hospital St.Gallen, St.Gallen, St.Gallen, Switzerland.\") , [Martin H Brutsche](/?term=Brutsche+MH&cauthor_id=34260151)  [1](#full-view-affiliation-1 \"Lung Center, Cantonal Hospital St.Gallen, St.Gallen, St.Gallen, Switzerland.\") , [Sieghart Sopper](/?term=Sopper+S&cauthor_id=34260151)  [2](#full-view-affiliation-2 \"Internal Medicine V, Medical University of Innsbruck, Innsbruck, Tyrol, Austria.\")   [3](#full-view-affiliation-3 \"Tyrolean Cancer Research Institute, Innsbruck, Tyrol, Austria.\")\n\nAffiliations Expand\n\n### Affiliations\n\n-   1 Lung Center, Cantonal Hospital St.Gallen, St.Gallen, St.Gallen, Switzerland.\n-   2 Internal Medicine V, Medical University of Innsbruck, Innsbruck, Tyrol, Austria.\n-   3 Tyrolean Cancer Research Institute, Innsbruck, Tyrol, Austria.\n-   4 Visceral, Transplant and Thoracic Surgery, Medical University of Innsbruck, Innsbruck, Tyrol, Austria.\n\n-   PMID: **34260151**\n-   PMCID: [PMC9292053](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9292053/)\n-   DOI: [10.1002/cyto.a.24481](https://doi.org/10.1002/cyto.a.24481)\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\n# OMIP 077: Definition of all principal human leukocyte populations using a broadly applicable 14-color panel\n\nMaximilian Boesch et al. Cytometry A. 2022 Jan.\n\nShow details\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat Abstract PubMed PMID\n\nCytometry A\n\nActions\n\n-   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22Cytometry+A%22%5Bjour%5D&sort=date&sort_order=desc)\n-   [Search in NLM Catalog](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nlmcatalog?term=%22Cytometry+A%22%5BTitle+Abbreviation%5D)\n-   [Add to Search](#)\n\n. 2022 Jan;101(1):15-20.\n\ndoi: 10.1002/cyto.a.24481. Epub 2021 Jul 14.\n\n### Authors\n\n[Maximilian Boesch](/?term=Boesch+M&cauthor_id=34260151)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Lung Center, Cantonal Hospital St.Gallen, St.Gallen, St.Gallen, Switzerland.\") , [Martina Sykora](/?term=Sykora+M&cauthor_id=34260151)  [2](#short-view-affiliation-2 \"Internal Medicine V, Medical University of Innsbruck, Innsbruck, Tyrol, Austria.\")   [3](#short-view-affiliation-3 \"Tyrolean Cancer Research Institute, Innsbruck, Tyrol, Austria.\") , [Silvia Gasteiger](/?term=Gasteiger+S&cauthor_id=34260151)  [3](#short-view-affiliation-3 \"Tyrolean Cancer Research Institute, Innsbruck, Tyrol, Austria.\")   [4](#short-view-affiliation-4 \"Visceral, Transplant and Thoracic Surgery, Medical University of Innsbruck, Innsbruck, Tyrol, Austria.\") , [Florent Baty](/?term=Baty+F&cauthor_id=34260151)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Lung Center, Cantonal Hospital St.Gallen, St.Gallen, St.Gallen, Switzerland.\") , [Martin H Brutsche](/?term=Brutsche+MH&cauthor_id=34260151)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"Lung Center, Cantonal Hospital St.Gallen, St.Gallen, St.Gallen, Switzerland.\") , [Sieghart Sopper](/?term=Sopper+S&cauthor_id=34260151)  [2](#short-view-affiliation-2 \"Internal Medicine V, Medical University of Innsbruck, Innsbruck, Tyrol, Austria.\")   [3](#short-view-affiliation-3 \"Tyrolean Cancer Research Institute, Innsbruck, Tyrol, Austria.\")\n\n### Affiliations\n\n-   1 Lung Center, Cantonal Hospital St.Gallen, St.Gallen, St.Gallen, Switzerland.\n-   2 Internal Medicine V, Medical University of Innsbruck, Innsbruck, Tyrol, Austria.\n-   3 Tyrolean Cancer Research Institute, Innsbruck, Tyrol, Austria.\n-   4 Visceral, Transplant and Thoracic Surgery, Medical University of Innsbruck, Innsbruck, Tyrol, Austria.\n\n-   PMID: **34260151**\n-   PMCID: [PMC9292053](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9292053/)\n-   DOI: [10.1002/cyto.a.24481](https://doi.org/10.1002/cyto.a.24481)\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\nFull text links Cite\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat AbstractPubMedPMID\n\n## Abstract\n\nThis Optimized Multicolor Immunofluorescence Panel was designed to identify and quantify all principal leukocyte populations in human blood using a minimum number of markers. We achieved this goal using a carefully selected combination of 14 surface markers compatible with standard flow cytometric instruments and accessible to a particularly large research community. Optimized for use in whole blood, this panel allows polymorphonuclear cell identification, supports live cell recovery, and is well-suited for absolute cell counting applications in the original in vivo volume. Panel performance and the separation of populations are high, and virtually no cells remain undefined after gating. Besides the identification of neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils, T cells, natural killer cells, B cells, plasma cells, monocytes, myeloid dendritic cells and plasmacytoid dendritic cells, this panel also covers progenitor cells and may therefore be attractive for stem cell researchers. Envisioned applications of this panel include immune monitoring within clinical trials, initial discovery to inform subset-targeted panels, and clinical diagnostics. In summary, this panel offers a broadly applicable platform for immune cell identification, quantification and characterization in human samples, particularly whole blood.\n\n**Keywords:** PBMC; absolute cell counting; flow cytometry; immune monitoring; immune phenotyping; leukocyte; whole blood.\n\n© 2021 The Authors. Cytometry Part A published by Wiley Periodicals LLC on behalf of International Society for Advancement of Cytometry.\n\n[PubMed Disclaimer](/disclaimer/)\n\n## Conflict of interest statement\n\nThe authors have no potential conflicts of interest to declare. No medical writer or other non‐author was involved in the preparation of the manuscript.\n\n## Figures\n\n [![FIGURE 1](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/7c61/9292053/9a54f604bd8b/CYTO-101-15-g001.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/7c61/9292053/a077fce5340b/CYTO-101-15-g001.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFIGURE 1\n\n**\n\nGating strategy and performance of…\n\n**\n\nFIGURE 1\n\n**\n\nGating strategy and performance of this OMIP. (A) General gating strategy for identifying…\n\n**FIGURE 1**\n\nGating strategy and performance of this OMIP. (A) General gating strategy for identifying single viable leukocytes in human WB. Note that the last gating step is only applicable when counting beads were included in the staining. (B) Exemplary gating strategy for identifying the principal cell populations downstream of the leukocyte denominator. (C) Opt‐SNE plot showing the separation of leukocyte populations in human WB. (D) Opt‐SNE plot showing the separation of PBMC populations in human WB. mDC, myeloid dendritic cell; MZB, marginal zone B; monocyte (c/nc), classical/nonclassical monocyte; NK, natural killer; NKT, natural killer T; OMIP, optimized multicolor immunofluorescence panel; PBMC, peripheral blood mononuclear cell; pDC, plasmacytoid dendritic cell; opt‐SNE, optimized t‐distributed stochastic neighbor embedding; WB, whole blood. *Note*: Data as shown here were acquired on a 3‐laser BD LSRFortessa flow cytometric instrument (BD biosciences) \\[Color figure can be viewed at wileyonlinelibrary.com\\]\n\n[See this image and copyright information in PMC](#)\n\n## References\n\n1.  1.  Liechti T, Roederer M. OMIP‐051 ‐ 28‐color flow cytometry panel to characterize B cells and myeloid cells. Cytometry A. 2019;95:150–5. - [PMC](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6546165/) - [PubMed](/30549419/)\n2.  1.  Park LM, Lannigan J, Jaimes MC. OMIP‐069: forty‐color full spectrum flow cytometry panel for deep Immunophenotyping of major cell subsets in human peripheral blood. Cytometry A. 2020;97:1044–51. - [PMC](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8132182/) - [PubMed](/32830910/)\n3.  1.  Bendall SC, Simonds EF, Qiu P, el Amir AD, Krutzik PO, Finck R, et al. Single‐cell mass cytometry of differential immune and drug responses across a human hematopoietic continuum. Science. 2011;332:687–96. - [PMC](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3273988/) - [PubMed](/21551058/)\n4.  1.  Boesch M, Zeimet AG, Reimer D, Schmidt S, Gastl G, Parson W, et al. The side population of ovarian cancer cells defines a heterogeneous compartment exhibiting stem cell characteristics. Oncotarget. 2014;5:7027–39. - [PMC](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4196181/) - [PubMed](/25216521/)\n5.  1.  Boesch M, Cosma A, Sopper S. Flow cytometry: to dump or not to dump. J Immunol. 2018;201:1813–5. - [PubMed](/30224359/)\n\nShow all 22 references\n\n## Publication types\n\n-   Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't\n    \n    Actions\n    \n    -   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22Research+Support%2C+Non-U.S.+Gov%27t%22%5Bpt%5D&sort=date&sort_order=desc)\n    -   [Search in MeSH](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/mesh?term=Research+Support%2C+Non-U.S.+Gov%27t)\n    -   [Add to Search](#)\n    \n\n## MeSH terms\n\n-   Dendritic Cells\n    \n    Actions\n    \n    -   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22Dendritic+Cells%22%5B